WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.400
If you’re anything like me,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.560
you have lots of ambitious
plans for the future.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.160
I’ve got a whole series of
new books I want to write,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.400
thousands more
videos to script.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.360
But guess what?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.760
None of us may be able to do
any of the things we want to do

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.240
if we don't have our health.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.480
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.960
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.560
There’s been some alarming news
about colon cancer recently.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.960
Did you know that colon cancer is the
#1 cancer killer among non-smokers?

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.400
And that people seem to be
getting it earlier and earlier in life?

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.080
Today, we look at the best
diet for a healthier colon.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.600
We have 100 trillion micro-organisms
residing in our gut,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.560
give or take a few trillion, but
“the spread of the Western lifestyle

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.840
has been accompanied
by microbial changes,”

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.880
which may be contributing to our
epidemics of chronic disease.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.320
The problem is that we’re
eating these meat-sweet diets,

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.160
characterized by a high intake
of animal products, sugars,

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.680
processed foods, and a low
intake of whole plant foods.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.000
Contrary to the fermentation of
the carbohydrates that make it down

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.720
to our colon—the fiber and
resistant starch that benefit us

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.840
through the generation of these
magical short chain fatty acids

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.120
like butyrate—microbial
protein fermentation,

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.240
when excess protein is consumed…
that generates potentially toxic

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.680
and pro-carcinogenic metabolites
involved in colorectal cancer.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.440
And so, what we eat
can cause an imbalance

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.600
in our gut microbiome and
potentially create a ‘recipe’

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.120
for colorectal cancer,
where a high-fat, high-meat,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.120
high processed food diet tips the scale

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.200
towards dysbiosis and
colorectal cancer,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.200
whereas a high-fiber and starch,
lower meat diet can pull you back

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.680
into symbiosis with your friendly
flora and away from cancer.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.920
We now have evidence from
interventional studies

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.320
suggesting that “adopting a plant-based,
minimally processed high-fiber diet

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.120
may rapidly reverse the effects of

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.040
meat-based diets on
the gut microbiome.”

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.760
So, what may be a new form of
personalized microbiome

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.360
medicine for chronic disease?

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.680
It’s called food, which can
rapidly and reproducibly

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.120
alter the human gut microbiome.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.480
Switch people between a whole
food plant-based diet

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.000
and more of an animal
food-based diet,

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.000
and you can see dramatic
shifts within two days,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.800
which can result in
toxic metabolites.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.800
Switch people to an
animal food-based diet

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.520
and levels of deoxycholic
acid go up,

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.120
which is a secondary bile
acid known to promote

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.080
DNA damage and liver cancers.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.120
Why do levels go up?

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.920
Because the bad bacteria
producing the stuff

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.040
triple—in just two days.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.120
And over time the richness
of the microbial diversity

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.303
in our gut is disappearing.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.840
“A low-fiber diet is a key
driver of microbiome depletion.”

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.240
Yeah, there’s antibiotics,
and cesarean sections,

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.400
and indoor plumbing,
but the only factor

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.920
that has been empirically
demonstrated to be important

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:13.760
is a diet low in MACs
(not Big Macs),

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.280
microbiota-accessible carbohydrates,

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.640
which is just a fancy name

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.960
for fiber found in a whole
plant foods and resistant starch

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.640
found mostly in beans, peas,
lentils, and whole grains.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.880
Our intake of dietary fiber,
our intake of whole plant foods,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.480
is negligibly low
in the Western world

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.800
when compared to what we evolved
to eat over millions of years.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.600
Such a low-fiber diet provides
insufficient nutrients for

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.640
our gut microbes, leading
not only to the loss

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:44.680
of bacterial diversity and richness,

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.840
but also to the reduction
in the production

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.360
of those beneficial
fermentation end products

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.160
that they make with the fiber.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.320
We are in effect, starving
our microbial self.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.920
What are we going to do about
the deleterious consequences

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.080
of a diet deficient
in whole plant foods?

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.680
Create new-fangled functional foods,
of course, and supplements,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.640
and drugs—prebiotics,
probiotics, synbiotics.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.360
Think how much money
there is to be made!

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:18.480
Or, we could just eat the way
our bodies were meant to eat.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.920
What kind of value is that going
to get your stockholders, though?

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:25.600
Don’t you know probiotic
pills may be the next

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.440
big source of big
pharma billions?

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.520
Why eat healthy though, when
you can just have someone else

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.440
eat healthy for you and
then get a fecal transplant

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.120
from a vegan!

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:41.720
Researchers compared the microbiomes
of vegans versus omnivores,

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.480
and found the vegan’s friendly
flora were churning out

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.120
more of the good stuff,
showing that a plant-based diet

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.280
may result in more beneficial
metabolites in the bloodstream

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.280
and less of the bad
stuff like TMAO.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.960
But while the impact
of a vegan diet

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.800
on what the bacteria
were making was large,

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:01.760
the effect on the composition
of the gut microbiome

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.040
was surprisingly modest.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.400
They only found “slight differences
between the gut microbiomes

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.320
of omnivores versus vegans”?

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.400
That was a shocker to the researchers;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.080
this very modest difference
juxtaposed against

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.560
the significantly enhanced
dietary consumption

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.640
of fermentable plant foods.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.320
The vegans were eating
nearly twice the fiber.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.520
Anyone see the problem here?

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:31.240
The vegans just barely made the
minimum daily intake of fiber.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:32.320
Why?

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.000
Because Oreos are vegan,
Cocoa Pebbles are vegan,

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.080
french fries, Coke, potato chips,
there are vegan Doritos and Pop-Tarts.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.520
You can eat a terrible vegan diet.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.080
Burkitt showed that you need
to get at least 50 grams a day

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.800
(of fiber) for colon
cancer prevention.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:53.800
That’s only half of what our
bodies were designed to get.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.640
We evolved getting
about 100 grams a day.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.640
And that’s what you see
in modern populations

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.560
that are immune to
epidemic colorectal cancer.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.160
So, what if instead of
feeding people a vegan diet,

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.360
you just fed people
that kind of diet,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:11.440
a diet centered around
whole plant foods?

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.880
We’ll find out next.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.880
Colon cancer is our second
leading cancer killer,

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.700
but some places,like rural Africa,

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.840
have more than 10 times
lower rates than we do.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.960
The reason we know it’s
not genetic is that

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.440
“migrant studies, such as
those in Japanese Hawaiians,

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.040
have demonstrated that it
only takes one generation

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:32.280
for the immigrant population

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.320
to assume the colon
cancer incidence

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:37.080
of the host Western population.”

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.400
Now, the change in
diet is considered

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.320
most probably responsible for this.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.080
But there are all
sorts of changes

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.040
when you move from
one culture to another—

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.760
like smoking rates, different
exposures to chemicals,

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:49.120
infections, antibiotics.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.840
You don’t know if it’s the diet…

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.867
until you put it to the test.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.760
This international group of researchers

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.320
were trying to find out
why colon cancer rates

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:02.240
were an order of
magnitude higher here—

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.720
in African Americans and Caucasians—

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:07.160
than in rural Africa.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:11.160
Well, if you look at American
colons, they’re a mess:

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.080
polyps, diverticulosis—
not to mention hemorrhoids—

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.640
whereas the African colons
were remarkably pristine.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.120
And more importantly,
sevenfold lower

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.680
colonic epithelial
proliferation rates,

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.480
a characteristic of
precancerous conditions.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.280
They measured everything
that they were eating

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.920
and concluded that the higher
colorectal cancer risk

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.680
and proliferation rates
were most closely

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.360
“associated with higher dietary
intakes of animal products”,

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.760
which may have led to
“higher colonic populations

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.640
of these potentially toxic [acid]

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.680
and bile-salt-producing bacteria.”

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.200
But you don’t know…

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.560
until you put it to the test.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.880
Higher rates are
associated with higher

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:54.480
animal protein and animal fat,

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:57.760
lower fiber consumption,
more of those bad bile acids,

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.920
less of those good short chain
fatty acids like butyrate,

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.160
and that higher
mucosal proliferation.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:06.440
But how do we know it’s the
diet that’s mucking things up?

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:07.160
You don’t know…

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:11.040
until you perform an
interventional study.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.960
How about we just
swap their diets?

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.760
Feed the Americans a high-
fiber African-style diet,

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.960
and those poor Africans get the SAD,

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.600
Standard American Diet,

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.320
like sausage and white flour
pancakes for breakfast,

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:24.920
a burger and fries for lunch,

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:28.200
and like some meatloaf
and white rice for supper.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:31.320
That was day one for the rural
Africans in the experiment,

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:33.760
whereas the Americans
were forced to eat

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.760
fruits and vegetables,
corn and beans.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.200
To help with compliance they
threw in more familiar foods

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.080
like veggie dogs, though
note it was not a vegan diet,

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.000
just generally plant-based.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.400
And the food exchanges
weren’t for years,

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.240
but just two weeks.

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.720
Could they see changes that fast?

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:54.600
The dietary changes
resulted in remarkable

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.280
reciprocal changes in the
lining of their colons

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:00.320
in terms of cancer risk
and their microbiome.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.600
Switching to plant-based boosted
the fiber fermentation

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.818
and suppressed the carcinogenic
bile acid synthesis.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.240
We know that when our friendly
flora ferment fiber,

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.400
they produce beneficial
compounds like butyrate,

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:14.840
which is anti-inflammatory
and anti-cancer.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:18.480
Well, impressively,
‘Africanization’ of the diet

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.680
more than doubled butyrate production,

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:23.880
whereas ‘Westernization’ cut it in half.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.920
In terms of toxic metabolites,
a significant drop on the healthier diet,

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.520
whereas the ‘meatloafy’
diet increased the levels

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:35.280
of these carcinogens by 400%
within just two weeks.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.640
So, bottom line (no pun intended):

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.200
what they were able to show is
that just by changing the food,

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.680
you can remarkably
change your risk.

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.840
In fact, that’s how the
lead investigator put it:

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.320
“change your diet,
change your cancer risk!”

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:54.640
It may be never too late
to start eating healthier.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:57.800
Based on these kind of data,
“adopting a whole-food vegan

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.520
or [even just] near-vegan diet
rich in fruits and vegetables,”

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:02.680
along with other healthy
lifestyle decisions,

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:04.840
“could have a stunningly
positive impact

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:07.160
on the cancer risks not
only of black Americans,

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:08.800
but of all peoples.”

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:11.520
“While it [might] be
unrealistic to expect

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:13.520
rapid and profound lifestyle changes

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.640
in the general population,
[hey]…at least we have

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:19.760
sound, effective advice to offer
to those who [make the choice]

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:24.840
to take the steps needed to
optimize their healthful longevity.”

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:25.880
In our next story,

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:30.080
we look at how modern African diets
may now be as miserably low in fiber

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.720
as American diets, but they still
have less cancer than we do.

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:37.320
Colorectal cancer is the
second leading cause

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:41.640
of cancer death in the United
States, after lung cancer.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.560
If you look at the rates of lung
cancer around the world,

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.800
they vary by a factor of 10.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.680
If there was nothing we could
do to prevent lung cancer,

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:53.360
if it just rose spontaneously,
happened at random,

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:56.280
you'd assume that the rates
everywhere would be the same,

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:59.400
but since there's such
a huge variation in rates

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.200
you assume there's
some external cause,

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:05.240
and indeed we now know
smoking is responsible

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:08.400
for 90% of lung cancer cases.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:11.560
So if we don't want to die
of the #1 cancer killer,

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:16.520
by just not smoking we can throw
90% of our risk out the window.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.200
For colon cancer there's
an even bigger spread,

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.160
bigger variation around the world.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.240
So it appears colon cancer
doesn't just happen,

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.640
something makes it happen.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.800
Well, if our lungs can get filled
with carcinogens from smoke,

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:36.400
maybe our colons are getting filled
with carcinogens from food.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.440
Researchers from the
University of Pittsburgh

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:42.200
and the University of Limpopo
sought to answer the question,

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:47.160
'Why do African Americans get more
colon cancer than native Africans?'

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.760
Why study Africans?

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:55.080
Because colon cancer is extremely
rare in native African populations,

00:11:55.080 --> 00:12:01.560
like more than 50 times lower than
rates of Americans, white or black.

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.800
It's the fiber, right?

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:07.200
Dr. Burkitt was the first to describe
the low incidence of colon cancer

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:10.560
in native Africans, ascribing it
to their traditional staple diet

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:13.920
that was high in whole grains
and, therefore, fiber content.

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:18.640
Seems you get about a 10% reduction
in risk for every 10 grams of fiber a day.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:23.000
So, hey, if it's a 1%
drop for each gram,

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:26.320
and they're eating upwards
of 100 grams a day,

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:31.360
well, that could explain why colon
cancer is so rare in sub-Saharan Africa.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:35.360
But wait a second, the
modern African diet

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:37.920
is highly processed,
low in fiber,

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:42.320
and yet there has been no dramatic
increase in colon cancer incidence.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.080
The modern African diet
has a low fiber content

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.360
as most populations
now depend on

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.960
commercially produced
refined cornmeal.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:53.520
And we're not just talking
low fiber intake,

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.200
we're talking United States
of America low,

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:00.840
down around half the
recommended daily allowance.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:06.640
Yet colon disease still remains rare,
still 50 times less colon cancer.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:10.560
Maybe it's because they're
thinner and exercise more?

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.560
No, they're not,
and no, they don't.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:17.720
If anything their physical activity
levels may now be even lower.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:22.520
So if they're sedentary like us,
eating mostly refined carbs,

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:26.400
few whole plant foods,
little fiber -- like us,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:30.560
why do they still have 50 times
less colon cancer than us?

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.040
Well, there is one difference.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.760
The diet of both African Americans
and Caucasian Americans

00:13:36.760 --> 00:13:41.040
is rich in meat, whereas
the native Africans' diet

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:45.880
is so low in meat and saturated fat
they have total cholesterol levels

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:51.240
averaging 139, compared
to over 200 in the U.S.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.280
So, yes, they don't get
a lot of fiber anymore,

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:59.080
but they continue to minimize
meat and animal fat consumption

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:02.120
supporting evidence that perhaps
the most powerful determinants

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:06.640
of colon cancer risk are the levels
of meat and animal fat intake.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:10.840
So why do Americans get more
colon cancer than Africans?

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:15.360
Maybe the rarity of colon cancer
in Africans is not the fiber,

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.600
but their low animal
product consumption.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.960
There is a divergence of opinion
as to whether it's the animal fat,

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.800
cholesterol, or animal protein
that's most responsible

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:27.520
for the increased cancer risk,
as all three have been shown

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:33.320
to have carcinogenic cancer causing
properties, but it may not really matter

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:39.280
which component is worse, as a diet
rich in one is usually rich in the others.

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:40.000
Finally today,

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:43.920
the fermentation of fiber in the gut may
help explain the dramatic difference

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:47.560
in colorectal cancer incidence
around the world.

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:50.760
More than 30 years ago,
an idea was put forward

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:55.200
that high colonic pH
promoted colorectal cancer.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:00.120
A high colonic pH may promote the
creation of carcinogens from bile acids,

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:05.160
a process that is inhibited once
you get below a pH of about 6.5.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.000
This is supported
by data like this,

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.920
showing those at higher risk for
colon cancer may have a higher stool pH,

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:13.668
and those at lower risk,
a lower pH.

00:15:13.668 --> 00:15:15.680
There was a dramatic difference
between the two groups,

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:19.800
with most of the high risk group,
pH over 8,

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:24.120
and most of the low risk group,
pH under 6.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:26.880
This may help explain
the 50-fold lower rates

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:30.520
of colon cancer in Africa
compared to America.

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:34.840
The bacteria we have in our gut
depends on what we eat.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:39.440
If we eat lots of fiber then we
preferentially feed the fiber eating bacteria,

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:42.200
which give us back all sorts
of health promoting substances

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:44.640
like short-chain fatty acids

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.720
that have anti-inflammatory
and anti-cancer properties.

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:51.760
More of these organic acids
in the stools of native Africans

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:54.240
than African Americans.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:56.720
More acids, so lower pH.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:01.160
Whereas putrefactive bacteria
eating animal protein

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:07.800
were able to INCREASE stool pH by
producing alkaline metabolites like ammonia.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:12.680
The pH of the stools of white versus
lack children in Africa was compared.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:16.920
Children, because you can more
readily sample their stools,

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:21.600
particularly the rural black school children
who were eating such high fiber diets—

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:24.560
whole grains, legumes, nuts,
vegetables, fruits and wild greens—

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:29.280
9 out of 10 could
produce a stool on demand.

00:16:29.280 --> 00:16:33.320
Stuffed from head to tail with plants they
could give you a stool sample at any time,

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:36.080
as easy as getting
a urine sample.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:38.600
Hard to even get access
to the white kids, though,

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:42.000
who were reluctant to
participate in such investigations,

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:45.040
even though they were given
waxed cartons fitted with lids,

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:51.200
and all the black kids got was
a plate, and a square of paper towel.

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:52.120
What’d they find?

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:57.000
Significantly lower fecal pH in those
eating the traditional rural plant-based diets,

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.520
compared to those eating
the western diet,

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.360
who were eating far fewer whole
plant foods than the black children.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:05.440
But remove some of
those whole plant foods,

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:07.960
like switch their corn for
white bread for just a few days,

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.920
and their stool pH goes up,

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:14.040
and add whole plant foods like an
extra 5 to 7 servings of fruit every day,

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:18.160
and their stool pH goes
down even further, gets more acidic.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:19.320
Makes sense, right?

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:22.320
What happens when you ferment plants,
fruits/veggies/grains?

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:26.320
They turn sour, like vinegars,
sauerkraut, sourdough,

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:31.320
because good bacteria like lactobacillus
produce organic acids like lactic acid.

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:37.480
And those who eat a lot of plants have
more of these good bugs in their system.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:43.040
So using the purple cabbage test,
we want blue pee, but pink poo.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:45.920
No surprise, then, if you
compare the fecal samples

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:49.920
of those eating vegetarian or vegan,
to those eating standard diets,

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:54.120
plant-based diets appear to shift
the makeup of the bacteria in our gut,

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.680
resulting in a significantly
lower stool pH,

00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:02.840
and the more plant-based,
the lower the pH dropped.

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:04.760
It’s like a positive
feedback loop.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:08.400
Fiber-eating bacteria produce
the acids to create the pH

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.520
at which fiber-eating
bacteria thrive

00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:16.280
while suppressing the group
of less beneficial bugs.

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:20.720
How long does it take to bring
stool pH down on a plant-based diet?

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:22.800
As little as two weeks.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:25.800
A dozen volunteers carefully
selected for their trustworthiness,

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:30.760
and randomized to sequentially go
on regular, vegetarian, or vegan diet,

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:35.040
and two weeks in, a
significant drop in fecal pH

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:38.640
was achieved eating
completely plant-based.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:42.080
But there are plant-based diets
and then there are plant-based diets.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:43.280
Remember these two groups?

00:18:43.280 --> 00:18:47.680
Dramatically different stool pH
yet both groups were vegetarian,

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:51.680
but the high risk group was eating
mostly refined grains, very little fiber,

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:54.760
whereas the low risk group was
eating whole grains and beans,

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:58.720
packed with fiber for our fiber-
friendly flora to munch on.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:02.520
Just as a reduction
of high serum cholesterol

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:05.320
contributes to the avoidance
of coronary heart disease,

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:10.680
so a fall in the fecal pH value may
contribute to the avoidance of bowel cancer,

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:17.005
and through the same means,
eating more whole plant foods.

